How does full-stack observability impact engineering speed, incident response=
, and cost control?
In this eBook from Datadog, you=E2=80=99ll learn how real teams across indust=
ries are using observability to:
Reduce mean time to resolution (MTTR)
Cut tooling costs and improve team efficiency
Align business and engineering KPIs
See how unifying your stack leads to faster troubleshooting and long-term ope=
rational gains.
Performance engineering is the discipline in which you design, analyze, and o=
ptimize software systems so that they run quickly. Also, they should use reso=
urces efficiently. In distributed architectures, the focus of any performance=
 engineering activity is on shaving milliseconds off response times. Or locat=
ing execution bottlenecks in CPU or memory. Or making sure infrastructure cos=
ts are kept under control.
Historically, performance engineering work has been slow. And manual. You oft=
en depend on a small group of really skilled developers. However, with the ri=
se of AI-assisted software development, there=E2=80=99s a contradiction. Deve=
lopers can write and ship code faster than ever. No matter what their skill l=
evel may be. But that code often introduces subtle performance problems.
To solve this problem, the Netflix engineering team has developed an autonomo=
us performance agent system. In this article, we=E2=80=99ll be learning more =
about it. Here=E2=80=99s what we will cover:
Bottlenecks for traditional performance work
The problem with AI-Generated code
Can LLMs handle profile data
The agentic profiling pipeline
The problems Netflix found in production
Safety, verification, and trust architecture
Shifting left
Disclaimer: This post is based on publicly shared details from various source=
s. References at the end. Please comment if you notice any inaccuracies.
Bottlenecks For Traditional Performance Work
Traditional performance engineering work at Netflix was built on a highly ite=
rative loop that is pretty much manual in nature.
First, you trigger profiling for an application. A developer tries to detect =
high latency or increased resource usage by triggering a profiler on a live p=
roduction server.
In the second step, the profiler outputs raw trace data or flame graphs. The =
developer downloads these details to their local machine.
Third, the developer spends time looking at complex, color-coded flame graphs=
. They try to spot wide stack frames that might show signs of higher-than-nor=
mal CPU or memory consumption.
Next, the developer goes through the codebase looking for cryptic method name=
s found in the flame graph. They try to make sense of the surrounding applica=
tion logic to figure things out.
Fifth, the developer traces the execution path and identifies the inefficient=
 operation. They write a proposed patch to fix the operation.
Moving further, the developers deploy the patch to an environment where any c=
hange in performance levels can be measured.
At the end, when new code paths are deployed in production, the developer sta=
rts the manual process once again.
As you may have noticed, this workflow doesn=E2=80=99t scale. Everything depe=
nds on a few domain experts who have a deep sense of understanding about low-=
level execution stacks. Since human attention is a scarce resource, you can i=
nvestigate performance issues only for major incidents or obvious system slow=
downs. Smaller but steady resource drains go unnoticed.
