The Problem with AI Code
The use of AI coding assistants has increased performance problems. There are=
 two main reasons for this. The speed trap and the context gap.
Developers who are using AI tools can generate and ship code up to 10 times f=
aster than before. No matter their skill level or knowledge of the system. Bu=
t you cannot guarantee that code that is fast to generate also performs well.=
 While your average developer might be able to write 10K lines of code in a f=
ew minutes, the team might have to spend years debugging regressions. Or deal=
ing with code bloat. Or handling other architecture-related problems.
Also, if you=E2=80=99ve noticed, LLMs are great at syntax and local-level log=
ic. If you ask an LLM to write a function or an API endpoint, it writes code =
that compiles successfully and runs without any obvious errors. It might also=
 pass standard unit tests. However, the LLM has no idea about the host platfo=
rm=E2=80=99s runtime environment.
For example, you can=E2=80=99t expect the LLM to know that a particular utili=
ty function being used in your code might run 10 million times per second on =
a critical request path. It cannot see the overall call stack. Neither can it=
 understand memory allocation challenges that might exist inside a container =
running at peak load within your platform. Therefore, the code you write migh=
t appear good enough in isolation. But it can still waste a huge amount of re=
sources when you run it at scale.
Can LLMs Handle Profile Data
Netflix wanted to test whether automation could remove these issues. They set=
 up an experiment to find out if an LLM can read and make sense of raw profil=
ing data directly.
Different languages have different profilers. But they use a shared language =
and store the same core information. For example, JVM=E2=80=99s async-profile=
r or Java Flight Recorder operates at 100 Hz sampling to capture execution tr=
aces, memory allocation rates, and thread lock contention. Python=E2=80=99s p=
y-spy provides low-overhead, async stack sampling of live production processe=
s. Go has pprof, which generates goroutine profiles, heap snapshots, and bloc=
k contention data.
Each one of these profilers breaks down execution into call stacks. They trac=
k two main metrics. First, you have the inclusive time. This is the total tim=
e spent executing a function. It includes every child function and the subrou=
tines called by this function. Second, you have the Self (CPU) Time. It=E2=80=
=99s the time spent running code purely inside the function itself. No extern=
al calls are involved.
With this exercise, the Netflix engineering team came to the conclusion that =
a single analytical pipeline could understand data from almost any programmin=
g runtime.
Another advantage of LLMs is that they don=E2=80=99t have to guess how an alg=
orithm works from scratch. Since LLMs have been broadly trained on many softw=
are repositories, they are mostly familiar with classic programming anti-patt=
erns. For example, they know that quadratic loops where a hot method is calle=
d again and again for each item in a collection aren=E2=80=99t a good idea. A=
lso, loop invariants where you compute a calculation for every iteration of t=
he loop instead of making the calculation once at the beginning. Other patter=
ns include excessive object allocation on hot execution paths and =E2=80=9CN+=
1=E2=80=9D query patterns.
Due to this knowledge base of LLMs, you don=E2=80=99t need to ask an AI model=
 to guess why an application might be performing poorly. You can build a work=
flow that asks the model to pattern-match known performance bugs against actu=
al call stacks.
The Agentic Profiling Pipeline
Netflix built an automated performance system that wraps the LLM inside a wel=
l-defined pipeline.
A key lesson Netflix engineers learnt during this implementation was about sc=
oping. If you feed an LLM a complete, raw stack trace without boundaries, it =
can get distracted. Often, the model spends a lot of reasoning tokens analyzi=
ng internal JVM garbage collection pauses, low-level Linux kernel syscalls, o=
r even 3rd party open-source framework overhead.
Application developers don=E2=80=99t have any control over such internal libr=
ary concerns or OS routines. Therefore, you need to provide the agent with st=
rict package prefix rules. This way, the model can ignore generic platform no=
ise and focus on tracing only the specific application code.
The Problems Netflix Found in Production
When the Netflix engineering team deployed the profiling pipeline against its=
 production service, the agent found 4 major categories of performance bugs.
First was an algorithmic bottleneck that wasted 8.8% of CPU resources. The ag=
ent found an aggregation method using Java Streams where an immutable map was=
 updated inside a reduction step.
// Anti-pattern: O(N^2) accumulation inside stream reduction
stream().reduce(ImmutableMap.of(), (acc, val) -> {
    return ImmutableMap.builder()
        .putAll(acc) // Copies every existing entry on every single step
        .put(val)
        .build();
});
Since putAll(acc) copies every existing key-value pair on every step, you nee=
d to copy elements again and again to build a map of size N. This results in =
quadratic complexity.
The agent modified the quadratic builder reduction with a linear collection a=
pproach.
// Optimized: O(N) single-pass collection
stream()
    .flatMap(e -> e.entrySet().stream())
    .collect(toMap(Entry::getKey, Entry::getValue));
The second category was about regular expressions on the hot path. In a servi=
ce, the developer had to sanitize incoming request strings by modifying chara=
cters that weren=E2=80=99t allowed. The earlier implementation ran a raw regu=
lar expression substitution on every request.
// Anti-pattern: Dynamic regex evaluation per request
String sanitized =3D input.replaceAll(=E2=80=9D[^a-zA-Z0-9.-]=E2=80=9D, =E2=
=80=9C_=E2=80=9D);
As you might know, evaluating regular expressions involves parsing and state-=
machine transitions. This logic on high-volume traffic used 46% of the servic=
e=E2=80=99s CPU time.
The agent replaced the regex evaluation with a pre-computed lookup table that=
 checked characters by index. The execution got 22 times faster.
The next category was related to unnecessary object allocation that caused OO=
M crashes. A service frequently checked whether incoming data payloads contai=
ned specific metadata fields. To perform the check, the code was creating a n=
ew, heavy HashSet on every incoming call.
However, the underlying data object already had an internal lookup method ava=
ilable. Also, instantiating millions of such objects consumed 47% of the serv=
ice=E2=80=99s total heap collections.
The agent fixed this issue by removing the redundant object instantiations an=
d modified the code to reuse the cached method.
The next category was around library misuse. For example, the discovery invol=
ved metric telemetry instrumentation. One of the services tracked operations =
by looking up a counter from a metric registry on every single call.
// Anti-pattern: Looking up metric instances on every function execution
Metrics.counter(=E2=80=9Drequest.count=E2=80=9D).increment();
When the query was sent to the registry, it needed dictionary lookups and int=
ernal synchronization locks. The profiler showed that the work consumed 66% o=
f the CPU, whereas the actual business logic used only 0.3%.
The agent fixed it by caching the metric counter reference at the time of cla=
ss initialization. Also, it found that the same issue was happening across th=
ree independent services.
Safety, Verification, and Trust Architecture
If you have an AI agent that can optimize code, it=E2=80=99s quite dangerous =
if the changes are applied without automatic verification. If the agent makes=
 a change that breaks business logic, it=E2=80=99s worse than inefficient cod=
e.
To prevent regressions, the agentic pipeline uses the standard SDLC.
In stage 1, you verify the logic. Before you move any code, the agent=E2=80=
=99s branch must pass all existing unit, integration, and contract tests. Thi=
s is to make sure that the changes made by the change don=E2=80=99t alter bas=
ic inputs, outputs, or handling of edge cases.
In stage 2, you have the canary deployment. If the tests pass, the pipeline s=
pins up a canary deployment in production. The canary instance gets a portion=
 of the traffic. It runs side-by-side with an identical baseline instance tha=
t runs the original code.
Stage 3 is about generating the observability report. The platform collects h=
ard telemetry metrics over a big enough window. Some metrics are relative CPU=
 utilization, median latency, P99 latency, and memory allocation rates. They =
also don=E2=80=99t use any AI model to check the canary results.
In stage 4, you have the final engineering decision. When the canary version =
shows a good level of performance improvement with zero increase in error rat=
es, the system opens a PR. This PR goes for human review. It includes the cod=
e change, the original flame graph, and the canary report. The final decision=
 on deploying the change to production is taken by the human developer.
Shifting Left
Automated production profiling is quite effective. But if you just rely on it=
, you end up exposing several limitations.
By the time an agent finds an anti-pattern in production, you=E2=80=99ve alre=
ady paid for days or weeks of wasted compute capacity.
Second, developers are anyway cautious about changing a running service. If y=
ou have a service that handles production traffic without crashing, the devel=
opers hesitate to just make changes for the sake of optimization.
Lastly, multiple teams often make the exact same mistakes when it comes to pa=
tterns. If you run a super-expensive agent on 10 different services just to f=
ind the same problem, it=E2=80=99s not very efficient.
These limitations led to Netflix making the decision to shift performance che=
cks left into the early development cycle. Also, they wanted the agents to sh=
are their findings across the entire engineering organization.
However, as you might know, language models are stateless. Every time you sen=
d a prompt to an LLM, it starts with a clean slate. It might no retain any me=
mory of the bug it found some time back in another service. The Netflix engin=
eering team built a Pattern Catalog to make the models achieve persistence. T=
his was done by pairing a stateless LLM with a stateful catalog. Or a shared =
memory across the fleet.
The Pattern Catalog was built around 4 key principles.
First, it has to be centralized and serve as the single source of truth for a=
ll engineering teams. This way, no two teams end up debugging the same issues.
The second principle is that the same catalog entries should work across dist=
inct environments. For example, offline profiling, CI/CD pull request checks,=
 and local development tools.
Third, you need to format entries so that an automation script can easily inj=
ect them into an LLM=E2=80=99s system prompt.
Lastly, the contents of the catalog should be reviewable by human developers.
To implement the Pattern Catalog, the Netflix engineering team used a simple =
Git repository filled with plain Markdown files. They didn=E2=80=99t use vect=
or databases or complex knowledge stores. The idea was borrowed from the =E2=
=80=9CLLM-wiki=E2=80=9D concept popularized by Andrej Karpathy.
By using a standard Git repository, the developers got several advantages. Fo=
r one, developers can diff changes, open pull requests, and use git features =
to find out why an entry was created. It also scales well to 100s of detailed=
 performance rules without the need for complex search infrastructure. Lastly=
, plain Markdown is easy for both humans and language models to read without =
translation layers.
How Netflix Catches Problems Early
With the Pattern Catalog in place, Netflix can handle performance engineering=
 via two paths. Reactive and Proactive.
In the reactive path, your system profiles production services on a recurring=
 schedule or on demand. When the agent finds a new bottleneck, it tests the f=
ix on a canary instance. If everything goes fine, the patch is merged, and th=
e agent documents the new finding in the Pattern Catalog.
In the proactive path, the process starts when a developer creates a new PR. =
CI tooling performs a fast regex or symbol match against the Pattern Catalog =
index. If the code in the PR matches known anti-patterns, the CI bot calls an=
 LLM and provides it with the pull request diff. The LLM checks the context a=
nd leaves an inline comment that explains the inefficiency. It shows the reso=
urce impact and suggests a fix. All this happens before the code ever merges =
to the main branch.
Conclusion
The key learning from the Netflix engineering experience is that automating p=
erformance engineering work is not an all-or-nothing game. You can have organ=
izations adopt workflows along a four-level maturity spectrum.
Level 0 is the manual situation. This is the level where machine learning is =
not used. Human developers manually capture traces, interpret flame graphs, a=
nd trace code paths.
Level 1 is the assist approach. This is the entry point for AI adoption. The =
LLM acts as an analytical copilot. A developer initiates the profiling proces=
s. But they don=E2=80=99t spend time studying flame graphs. Instead, they fee=
d the stack dump to the model. The model finds the hot execution path.
Level 2 is the orchestration level. This is the current state at Netflix. A l=
ot of proactive scanning happens. Also, they use the Pattern Catalog.
Level 3 is the fully autonomous level. This is the long-term vision at Netfli=
x for performance agents. At Level 3, the agents will create their own optimi=
zation strategies. They will explore multiple alternative implementations. Or=
 run automated benchmarks.
