p style=3D"margin: 0 0 20px 0;color: rgb(54,55,55);line-height: 26px;font-siz=
e: 16px;unicode-bidi: plaintext;">The key learning from the Netflix engineeri=
ng experience is that automating performance engineering work is not an all-o=
r-nothing game. You can have organizations adopt workflows along a four-level=
 maturity spectrum.</p><p style=3D"margin: 0 0 20px 0;color: rgb(54,55,55);li=
ne-height: 26px;font-size: 16px;unicode-bidi: plaintext;">Level 0 is the manu=
al situation. This is the level where machine learning is not used. Human dev=
elopers manually capture traces, interpret flame graphs, and trace code paths=
.</p><p style=3D"margin: 0 0 20px 0;color: rgb(54,55,55);line-height: 26px;fo=
nt-size: 16px;unicode-bidi: plaintext;">Level 1 is the assist approach. This =
is the entry point for AI adoption. The LLM acts as an analytical copilot. A =
developer initiates the profiling process. But they don&#8217;t spend time st=
udying flame graphs. Instead, they feed the stack dump to the model. The mode=
l finds the hot execution path.</p><p style=3D"margin: 0 0 20px 0;color: rgb(=
54,55,55);line-height: 26px;font-size: 16px;unicode-bidi: plaintext;">Level 2=
 is the orchestration level. This is the current state at Netflix. A lot of p=
roactive scanning happens. Also, they use the Pattern Catalog.</p><p style=3D=
"margin: 0 0 20px 0;color: rgb(54,55,55);line-height: 26px;font-size: 16px;un=
icode-bidi: plaintext;">Level 3 is the fully autonomous level. This is the lo=
ng-term vision at Netflix for performance agents. At Level 3, the agents will=
 create their own optimization strategies. They will explore multiple alterna=
tive implementations. Or run automated benchmarks.</p><p style=3D"margin: 0 0=
